---
import BaseLayout from '~/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Figure from '~/components/Figure.astro';
import Assumptions from '~/components/Assumptions.astro';
import Callout from '~/components/Callout.astro';
import Math from '~/components/Math.astro';
import Badge from '~/components/Badge.astro';
import Cite from '~/components/Cite.astro';
import HarmonicsLab from '~/islands/HarmonicsLab';
import TensorProductLab from '~/islands/TensorProductLab';

const shAssumptions = [
  { term: 'Functions', value: 'Real spherical harmonics, orthonormal on the unit sphere, Condon–Shortley phase omitted. Libraries such as e3nn use their own sign and ordering conventions; the invariants shown do not depend on them.' },
  { term: 'Polar plot', value: 'The surface point in direction r̂ sits at distance |Y_lm(r̂)| (scaled to fit); colour gives the sign.' },
  { term: 'Neighbour', value: 'Only its direction r̂ matters here; in a real model a learned radial function of the distance multiplies each block.' },
];
const tpAssumptions = [
  { term: 'Group', value: 'O(3): proper rotations plus inversion. Inputs have natural parity (−1)^l, as spherical harmonics of bond directions do.' },
  { term: 'Counting', value: 'Each output degree appears once; real networks keep a subset of paths and give each its own learned weights.' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Spherical harmonics and tensor products"
  description="Physics Lab P5: how equivariant MLIPs turn a neighbour direction into features that rotate correctly — spherical harmonics, the invariant norm of each block, and the selection rules of tensor products."
>
  <article class="page-grid lab-page">
    <header class="wide head">
      <p class="eyebrow"><a href="/physics-lab/">Physics Lab</a> · P5</p>
      <h1 class="h-display">Spherical harmonics and tensor products</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        An equivariant network has to describe <em>where</em> a neighbour is without picking a favourite direction in
        space. Spherical harmonics turn a direction into a set of numbers that rotate in a controlled way, and tensor
        products combine those sets without breaking the rules of <a href="/physics-lab/rotation/">P4</a>.
      </p>
      <div class="head-badges">
        <Badge label="live-calculation" />
        <Badge label="theory" />
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="sh-h">
      <h2 id="sh-h" class="h-section">1 · A direction as a feature</h2>
      <p>
        For a bond direction <b>r̂</b>, the degree-<i>l</i> block is the list of 2<i>l</i> + 1 numbers
        <Math tex={String.raw`\mathbf Y_l(\hat{\mathbf r}) = \big(Y_{l,-l}(\hat{\mathbf r}),\dots,Y_{l,l}(\hat{\mathbf r})\big)`} />.
        Degree 0 is a single constant, degree 1 is the direction itself, and higher degrees describe finer angular
        detail. Rotating the direction mixes the components of a block by a fixed orthogonal matrix
        <Math tex={String.raw`D^{l}(Q)`} />, the Wigner matrix, and never mixes blocks of different <i>l</i>:
      </p>
      <Math display number="1" tex={String.raw`\mathbf Y_l(Q\hat{\mathbf r}) = D^{l}(Q)\,\mathbf Y_l(\hat{\mathbf r}), \qquad \sum_{m=-l}^{l} Y_{lm}(\hat{\mathbf r})^2 = \frac{2l+1}{4\pi}\ \text{ for every } \hat{\mathbf r}`} />
      <p>
        The second identity, the addition theorem, is the invariant: the length of each block cannot change under rotation.
        Pick a block and move the neighbour around to watch both halves of (1) at once.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="wide fig-wrap">
      <Figure title="Real spherical harmonics and the feature block of a neighbour direction" labels={['live-calculation', 'theory']} number={1} id="fig-harmonics" framed={false}>
        <HarmonicsLab client:visible />
        <Fragment slot="caption">
          Left: Y<sub>lm</sub> drawn as a polar surface (teal positive, amber negative) with a neighbour on the grey
          sphere. Right: the 2<i>l</i> + 1 components of the degree-<i>l</i> block for the neighbour’s direction, and their
          sum of squares relative to (2<i>l</i>+1)/4π, which stays at 1 wherever the neighbour goes.
        </Fragment>
        <Assumptions slot="assumptions" items={shAssumptions} />
        <Fragment slot="source">Standard definitions; evaluated exactly in your browser and checked in the Atlas test suite (orthonormality, parity, addition theorem).</Fragment>
      </Figure>
    </div>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="tp-h">
      <h2 id="tp-h" class="h-section">2 · Combining two blocks</h2>
      <p>
        A message in an equivariant network multiplies features: the neighbour’s current features with the spherical
        harmonics of the bond. Multiplying two blocks gives (2<i>l</i><sub>1</sub>+1)(2<i>l</i><sub>2</sub>+1) numbers that
        do not rotate as one block, but they split exactly into blocks that do, using Clebsch–Gordan coefficients
        <Math tex={String.raw`C`} />:
      </p>
      <Math display number="2" tex={String.raw`l_1 \otimes l_2 = \bigoplus_{l=|l_1-l_2|}^{l_1+l_2} l, \qquad (\mathbf x \otimes \mathbf y)_{lm} = \sum_{m_1 m_2} C^{lm}_{l_1 m_1,\, l_2 m_2}\; x_{l_1 m_1}\, y_{l_2 m_2}`} />
    </section>

    <div class="wide fig-wrap">
      <Figure title="Which blocks can two blocks make?" labels={['live-calculation', 'theory']} number={2} id="fig-tensor" framed={false}>
        <TensorProductLab client:visible />
        <Fragment slot="caption">
          Each square is one component. The inputs’ components are redistributed over the outputs with none left over;
          striped outputs have the opposite parity to an ordinary block of that degree (pseudo-scalars, pseudovectors…).
        </Fragment>
        <Assumptions slot="assumptions" items={tpAssumptions} />
        <Fragment slot="source">Clebsch–Gordan decomposition of O(3) representations, as used by e3nn <Cite id="geiger2022e3nn" />.</Fragment>
      </Figure>
    </div>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="mlip-h">
      <h2 id="mlip-h" class="h-section">Where this sits inside a model</h2>
      <p>Schematically, an equivariant message from atom <i>j</i> to atom <i>i</i> is a learned radial function of the distance times a tensor product:</p>
      <Math display number="3" tex={String.raw`\mathbf m_{ij} = R\big(r_{ij}\big)\; \mathbf Y\big(\hat{\mathbf r}_{ij}\big) \otimes \mathbf h_j`} />
      <p>
        Every term in (3) rotates correctly by construction, so the energy read out from the degree-0 features is
        invariant and the forces are equivariant, exactly as derived in <a href="/physics-lab/rotation/#derivation">P4</a>.
        NequIP builds its interactions this way <Cite id="batzner2022nequip" />; MACE adds higher body order by taking
        repeated products of the atom-centred features <Cite id="batatia2022mace" />.
      </p>
      <Callout kind="caveat" title="What this page does not show">
        Equation (3) is a schematic. Real architectures differ in which paths they keep, how radial functions and
        weights are parameterised, and how features are normalised; those details live on each model’s page.
      </Callout>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .lab-page {
    padding-block: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 4rem) clamp(3rem, 6vw, 5rem);
  }
  .head .eyebrow a {
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .head h1 {
    margin-block: 0.6rem 1rem;
    font-size: clamp(2.1rem, 4.4vw, 3.6rem);
    line-height: 1.05;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
  .lede {
    font: 1.1875rem/1.6 var(--font-serif);
    color: var(--atlas-ink-muted);
    max-width: 44rem;
  }
  .lede a,
  .block a {
    color: var(--atlas-arch);
  }
  .head-badges {
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.35rem;
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }
  .block {
    margin-top: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3rem);
    font: 1.0625rem/1.7 var(--font-serif);
    color: var(--atlas-ink);
  }
  .block p {
    margin-block: 0 1rem;
  }
  .fig-wrap {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }
</style>
